**Flexbox is one-dimensional: it lays items out along a single row or column and lets content size drive the layout. Grid is two-dimensional: you define rows and columns and place items into them, so the layout drives the content.**

**Reach for flexbox when**
- Aligning items in a single line: navbars, toolbars, button groups, a card's footer.
- You want items to size to their content and share leftover space (`flex: 1`).
- Wrapping is okay to be ragged (each wrapped line is laid out independently).

**Reach for grid when**
- You need things to line up in both directions: page shells, dashboards, image galleries, forms with aligned labels.
- You want explicit tracks, gaps and named areas.
- You want a responsive grid without media queries.

```css
/* Flex: toolbar with a spacer */
.toolbar { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; }
.toolbar .spacer { flex: 1; }

/* Grid: responsive cards, no media queries */
.cards {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(220px, 1fr));
  gap: 16px;
}
```

**Subgrid** (`grid-template-rows: subgrid`) lets nested items align to the parent's tracks, e.g. card titles, bodies and buttons lining up across a row of cards of different lengths. That used to require fixed heights or JavaScript.

**In practice** you combine them: grid for the page and the card list, flex inside each card. A useful test: if the last row of wrapped items should align with the columns above, it is a grid; if it should just flow, it is flex.

**The modern answer is grid or flex on the parent: `display: grid; place-items: center;` centers a child in both axes. Which technique is right depends on whether you are centering text, a block of known width, or something positioned on top of other content.**

```css
/* 1. Grid: shortest */
.parent { display: grid; place-items: center; min-height: 100dvh; }

/* 2. Flex */
.parent { display: flex; justify-content: center; align-items: center; }

/* 3. Auto margins inside flex or grid */
.parent { display: flex; }
.child { margin: auto; }

/* 4. Overlay on top of other content */
.modal { position: fixed; inset: 0; margin: auto; width: 400px; height: fit-content; }

/* 5. Absolute + transform (unknown size, legacy) */
.child { position: absolute; top: 50%; left: 50%; transform: translate(-50%, -50%); }
```

**Other cases**
- **Inline content / text:** `text-align: center` on the parent.
- **A block of fixed width horizontally:** `margin-inline: auto`.
- **Block layout, no flex/grid:** `align-content: center` now works on plain block containers in modern browsers.

**Gotchas**
- Vertical centering needs the parent to have height; a parent that shrinks to its content has nothing to center within (hence `min-height`).
- `translate(-50%, -50%)` can land on half pixels and blur text.
- With flex centering, content taller than the container overflows on *both* sides and the top becomes unscrollable; `justify-content: safe center` or `margin: auto` avoids that.

**`display: none` removes the element from layout and the accessibility tree. `visibility: hidden` keeps its space but hides it and removes it from the accessibility tree and interaction. `opacity: 0` keeps space and stays fully interactive and readable by screen readers, it is just transparent.**

| | Takes space | Clickable / focusable | Screen reader | Animatable |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `display: none` | No | No | Hidden | Not with plain transitions |
| `visibility: hidden` | Yes | No | Hidden | Yes (flips at end) |
| `opacity: 0` | Yes | Yes | Announced | Yes, smoothly |

**Mechanism**
- `display: none` skips the element (and children) in the render tree, so changing it triggers layout.
- `visibility` is inherited, and a child can override it with `visibility: visible`; `display: none` cannot be overridden by children.
- `opacity` is composited on the GPU, so fading is cheap, but the invisible element can still swallow clicks.

```css
/* Fade out without leaving a clickable ghost */
.toast { transition: opacity .2s, visibility .2s; }
.toast.hidden { opacity: 0; visibility: hidden; }

/* Visually hidden but still read by screen readers */
.sr-only {
  position: absolute; width: 1px; height: 1px; overflow: hidden;
  clip-path: inset(50%); white-space: nowrap;
}
```

**Modern note:** transitions to and from `display: none` are now possible with `transition-behavior: allow-discrete` and `@starting-style`. The `hidden` attribute is `display: none`; `hidden="until-found"` keeps content findable by Ctrl+F.

**Build mobile-first with fluid layouts (grid, flex, `clamp()`, relative units), then add breakpoints only where the design breaks. Media queries respond to the viewport; container queries respond to the size of a component's container, so a component adapts wherever it is placed.**

**Mobile-first**
- Base styles target small screens; `@media (min-width: ...)` layers on enhancements.
- Small screens get the least CSS and the simplest layout, which tends to be faster and easier to maintain.
- Many layouts need no breakpoint at all: `repeat(auto-fit, minmax(16rem, 1fr))`, `flex-wrap`, `clamp()` for type.

```css
.card { display: grid; gap: 1rem; }

/* Media query: page-level layout */
@media (min-width: 64rem) {
  .page { grid-template-columns: 16rem 1fr; }
}

/* Container query: the card adapts to its slot */
.card-slot { container-type: inline-size; }
@container (min-width: 30rem) {
  .card { grid-template-columns: 8rem 1fr; }
}
```

**Why container queries matter:** the same card might sit in a wide main column and a narrow sidebar on the same viewport. A media query cannot tell those apart; a container query can. Container query units (`cqi`, `cqw`) let type scale with the component.

**Still use media queries for** page shell layout, and for user preferences: `prefers-color-scheme`, `prefers-reduced-motion`, `hover`, `pointer: coarse`.

**Gotchas**
- `container-type: inline-size` applies size containment, so the container cannot size itself from its children's width.
- An element cannot query itself; it queries its nearest container ancestor.
- Do not forget the viewport meta tag: `width=device-width, initial-scale=1`.

**A pseudo-class (single colon, like `:hover`, `:focus-visible`, `:nth-child()`) selects an existing element in a particular state or position. A pseudo-element (double colon, like `::before`, `::placeholder`) styles a part of an element or generated content that is not in the DOM. `:has()` is a relational pseudo-class: it selects an element based on its descendants or following siblings, the long-missing "parent selector".**

**Pseudo-classes:** state (`:hover`, `:checked`, `:disabled`, `:user-invalid`), structure (`:first-child`, `:nth-of-type()`), logic (`:is()`, `:where()`, `:not()`).

**Pseudo-elements:** `::before`/`::after` (need `content`), `::first-line`, `::marker`, `::selection`, `::backdrop`. They are not focusable or selectable by JavaScript queries, and screen readers may read generated `content`, so keep it decorative.

```css
/* Style a card differently if it contains an image */
.card:has(img) { grid-template-rows: 12rem auto; }

/* Highlight a field wrapper when its input is invalid after interaction */
.field:has(input:user-invalid) { border-color: var(--danger); }

/* Disable page scroll while a dialog is open */
body:has(dialog[open]) { overflow: hidden; }

/* Previous-sibling style: the label before a focused input */
label:has(+ input:focus-visible) { color: var(--accent); }
```

**Why :has() is a big deal:** it replaces JavaScript that toggled classes on parents (form validation wrappers, "has image" variants, layout changes when a sidebar is present). It is supported in all modern browsers.

**Gotchas**
- `:has()` takes the specificity of its most specific argument.
- Very broad selectors like `*:has(...)` on large DOMs can be costly to re-evaluate; scope them.
- Prefer `:focus-visible` over `:focus` for focus rings so mouse clicks do not show them.
